(
    version: 1,
    id: "m01",
    title: "First Drive",
    chapter: 1,
    concepts: ["Actuators", "Power"],
    world: (id: "test_track"),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 10.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.9, 0.9),
    brief_md: r#"
## Get the Rover to the 1 m line

The test track has a mark every 100 mm. Drive the Rover forward and stop it so that the **front of
the Rover** (the front of its wheels, 48 mm in front of the hub) ends between the 950 mm and the
1050 mm marks.

You have **10 seconds**. A robot that is still rolling when the time is up does not count.

*Hint for thinking:* a motor only gets power while you tell it to. What decides when you stop?
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "The Rover drives with both motors at power 0.5 for 2 seconds. How far will it travel?",
        unit: "mm",
        measured: "drive_half_power_2s_mm",
    )),
    criteria: [FrontStop(min_x: 0.95, max_x: 1.05, within_s: 10.0)],
    stars: (
        (text: "Stop within 20 mm of the 1 m mark", criteria: [FrontStop(min_x: 0.98, max_x: 1.02, within_s: 10.0)]),
        (text: "Do it without opening a hint", no_hints: true),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Power is not distance. Run once, look at where the Rover stops, and change one thing.",
        "The wheel encoder counts how far the wheel has turned: axl_motor_angle(left) in radians. A wheel of radius r that turns by an angle a rolls r times a.",
        "Drive with axl_motor_power until the average of the two angles times the wheel radius (0.028 m) is the distance you want, then call axl_motor_brake on both motors and keep calling axl_step until the end.",
    ),
)
